  "Q16": {
    "answer": "(a) Lin's Form I-94 should show an admit-until date of September 22, 2031. Arithmetic: Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) and (f)(5)(v) (91 FR 45124-45125) and preamble (91 FR 45016, 45098), her August 2, 2027 entry within the 30-day pre-program window does not count toward the 4-year maximum; the 4-year maximum runs from her program start date (August 23, 2027) to August 23, 2031, plus an additional 30 days for departure preparation: 8 remaining days in August (Aug 24-31) + 22 days in September = September 22, 2031. (b) To stay beyond September 22, 2031, Lin must file an EOS application (Form I-539) with USCIS on or before September 22, 2031 (or before August 23, 2031 to preserve automatic extension of any on-campus/CPT employment) under 8 CFR 214.1(c)(2) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iii)(B). She must submit the designated EOS form, required fee, her updated/properly endorsed Form I-20 showing the May 14, 2033 program end date, evidence of sufficient funds to cover expenses, and any biometrics required under 8 CFR 103.16 (along with proof of maintaining status and full-time study) (8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)-(iii)). It can be granted under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)(C)(1) based on \"[a] currently issued Form I-20, or successor form, indicating additional time is left to complete his or her program of study\" (preamble 91 FR 45040, 45108-45109 also references compelling academic reasons for standard programs exceeding 4 years). How early she may file: there is no regulatory prohibition on how early Form I-539 may be filed ahead of expiration (91 FR 45040 n.197, 45107 n.328, 45110 n.338), though DHS generally recommends filing within the 6 months preceding expiration (91 FR 45043, 45106-45107, 45110). If granted in full (through May 14, 2033), under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(vi) and (f)(5)(v) she may stay until May 14, 2033 plus 30 days: 17 remaining days in May (May 15-31) + 13 days in June = June 13, 2033. (c) No. In the preamble at 91 FR 45042-45043, DHS explicitly confirmed that a student issued a Form I-20 for a PhD program as the primary academic objective may obtain a master's degree incidental to status while progressing toward completing the PhD; 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(ii)(C) bars only starting a new program at the same or lower level after completing a program. (d) When Lin departs and seeks readmission in August 2030 (with no EOS pending) on her valid Form I-20 showing May 14, 2033 (less than 4 years away), CBP may admit her under 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i) (as explained in preamble at 91 FR 45098) up to her May 14, 2033 program end date plus 30 days = June 13, 2033; if CBP admits her through June 13, 2033, she would no longer need the EOS filing in (b). Certainty: Moderately certain, subject to three caveats: (1) CBP admission and exact admit-until period (\"may be admitted\" / \"up to\") are discretionary; (2) if her three-week trip is solely to contiguous territory or adjacent islands and she relies on automatic visa revalidation under 8 CFR 214.1(b)(1), CBP admits only up to the previously authorized unexpired stay (Sept. 22, 2031); and (3) textually, 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A) refers to admission \"after a previously authorized period of admission ... expired\" while (i)(B) covers returns with a pending EOS, so returning without a pending EOS before prior stay expires relies on the preamble's explicit statement at 91 FR 45098 that returning aliens without an EOS may be admitted for the remaining I-20 length up to 4 years plus 30 days. (e) Even if her husband's I-94 shows a later date, he may stay only as long as Lin's authorized period of stay (September 22, 2031 unless/until Lin obtains a later authorized stay). Under 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i)(E) (91 FR 45124), an F-2 dependent's authorized stay may not exceed the principal F-1 student's authorized stay, and preamble at 91 FR 45016 confirms that dependents cannot stay longer than the principal even if the F-2's I-94 reflects a longer end date. When Lin applies under (b), under 8 CFR 214.1(c)(2) and 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(iv) (91 FR 45123, 45126), her husband may either be included on Lin's Form I-539 application (by filing Form I-539A) or file his own separate Form I-539 (with proof of relationship, maintenance of status, no unauthorized work, updated I-20, funds, and biometrics if required). On the fee: the preamble at 91 FR 45013 and 45110 states that if the dependent files Form I-539A as part of the principal's Form I-539 request, only one Form I-539 filing fee is required, whereas if the dependent files a separate Form I-539, a separate filing fee is required.",
    "basis": "Final rule 2026-14439: 8 CFR 214.1(a)(4)(i)(A), (b)(1), and (c)(2) (91 FR 45122-45123); 8 CFR 214.2(f)(5)(i), (f)(5)(i)(E), (f)(5)(ii)(C), and (f)(5)(v) (91 FR 45124-45125); 8 CFR 214.2(f)(7)(i)-(iv) and (vi) (91 FR 45126); and preamble at 91 FR 45013, 45016, 45040 & n.197, 45042-45043, 45098, 45106-45107, 45108-45110.",
